=== j-boundary-implementation.md: 5-factor record + AST-insufficiency (lines 68-105) ===
68: Prepare a target-owned decision before rendering a conversion. It records:
69: 
70: 1. Source container identity and element type from the typed Haxe input.
71: 2. The prepared operand's actual Swift container and optional representation.
72: 3. The applicable flow fact and the operand to which that fact applies.
73: 4. Destination container, element, and optional requirements.
74: 5. The selected operation, required operand evaluation, and result representation.
75: 
76: Use a small typed result with explicit alternatives and an immutable result
77: type. Document the producer of each fact. An AST type is insufficient when
78: a previous conversion or narrowing has changed the emitted representation.
79: Record a completed conversion so a later consumer can use its result directly.
80: 
81: Keep expression results, binding representations, and flow facts distinct.
82: A null initializer describes that expression's value; a nullable array binding
83: still has a declared container representation when present. Reads use that
84: binding representation and the flow facts valid at the read. Initializer facts
85: cannot be cached as the binding's permanent value state. The parameter
86: declaration producer must also provide its selected representation to body
87: lowering, including whether a default has already supplied a required value.
88: Assignment, branch exit, and captured mutation must respect each fact's
89: invalidation rule.
90: 
91: The operand producer returns emitted text with its established representation.
92: Use the following provenance requirements when reviewing that producer:
93: 
94: | Operand operation | Evidence for the returned representation |
95: | --- | --- |
96: | Primitive local, field, or parameter read | The declaration's target mapping and any applicable flow fact agree with the selected read operation. |
97: | Literal construction | The selected construction determines storage and contextual element type. |
98: | Completed conversion | The conversion's result type supplies the representation for the next consumer. |
99: | Guarded extraction | The valid guard applies to this operand and the selected extraction produces a required value. |
100: | Default selection or branch merge | The selected lowering reconciles both alternatives and preserves lazy evaluation; the existence of default metadata alone proves no materialization. |
101: 
102: Keep operation selection and its returned facts together. A separate predictor
103: that guesses what a later renderer will select creates a second decision owner.
104: An unavailable optional optimization must retain the valid general lowering.
105: If accepted source has no valid lowering, report the missing rule for review.
